Ladybird skips per-frame sampling of forward infinite animations inside
box-less iframe containers. Script observations still sample current
time, and CSS iteration listeners keep their scheduled wake-ups. This
optimization leaves finite and reverse-playing animations on their
normal sampling paths so they can still complete while hidden.

Blink’s FrameView::CanThrottleRenderingForPropagation() accounts for
cross-origin frames whose OwnerLayoutObject() is absent. Gecko sets its
IsUnderHiddenEmbedderElement flag from the embedding layout, through
nsSubDocumentFrame::PropagateIsUnderHiddenEmbedderElement(). Gecko’s
PresShell::IsVisible() and Document::ShouldThrottleFrameRequests() both
consult that flag when deciding if a document can be rendered.

WebKit’s KeyframeEffect::ticksContinuouslyWhileActive() skips effects
whose target has neither a renderer nor a retained style, and its
KeyframeEffect::timeToNextTick() schedules observable CSS iteration
events at iteration boundaries. FrameView::invalidateRect() stops
repaint propagation when the iframe owner has no renderer to repaint.

The engines differ in their frame-level throttling policies. Ladybird
keeps its frame-level timer and requestAnimationFrame behavior, and
extends its effect-level sampling optimization across local iframe
containers. This follows the same observation-preserving approach as
WebKit’s per-effect scheduling, with an additional embedding-box check.
